DOMAIN: The Central Debate -- Appropriationism vs. Contextualism
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-01
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 10, Medium: 6, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The central methodological debate in the historiography of philosophy
divides between two poles that have been labelled "appropriationism"
(or "rational reconstruction") and "contextualism" (or "historical
reconstruction"). Appropriationists, typified by Richard Rorty's
programmatic essay "The Historiography of Philosophy: Four Genres"
(1984) and exemplified in the practice of Jonathan Bennett's
reconstructive readings of early modern figures, treat past
philosophers as conversation partners whose positions can be freely
recast in contemporary terms, correcting errors and extracting
insights for live philosophical debates. The criterion of success is
philosophical illumination rather than historical accuracy. By
contrast, contextualists, led paradigmatically by Quentin Skinner's
1969 manifesto "Meaning and Understanding in the History of Ideas,"
insist that the meaning of a philosophical text is fixed by its
linguistic and intellectual context of utterance; to understand a
past thinker is to recover what that thinker was doing in saying what
they said, not to project present problems onto past texts.

The debate entered its contemporary phase most sharply in the 2015
exchange in the Journal of the History of Philosophy between Daniel
Garber (contextualist) and Michael Della Rocca (appropriationist) on
interpreting Spinoza, and in Christia Mercer's 2019 retrospective on
"the contextualist revolution" in early modern studies. Recent work
(Corkum 2023; Hangai 2023; Blau 2020; Ferreira da Silva 2022) has
continued to probe the binary, with several authors proposing hybrid
or "third-way" positions that preserve some normative role for
philosophical reasoning without abandoning contextual sensitivity.
Mogens Laerke, Justin Smith, and Eric Schliesser have edited two
significant volumes (2013, 2022) that explicitly aim to move beyond
the binary by articulating a plurality of methods.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain furnishes the foundational conceptual materials for the
entire review: the key positions, canonical arguments, and principal
texts on both sides of the debate are catalogued here, providing the
synthesis writer with the intellectual architecture of the
appropriationism/contextualism debate. The anchor volume (Smith,
Schliesser, Laerke, eds., The Methodology of the History of
Philosophy, OUP) belongs to this domain's bibliography and connects
directly to the other domain reviews.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Systematic engagement between Skinner's speech-act framework (Austin/
Grice lineage) and philosophy of language perspectives on textual
meaning remains underexplored; most philosophical responses to Skinner
engage his conclusions without engaging the linguistic theory. Jonathan
Bennett's books (A Study of Spinoza's Ethics, 1984; Locke Berkeley
Hume, 1971) appear in the secondary literature but cannot be fully
verified via CrossRef due to age. The Methodology of the History of
Philosophy (Smith, Schliesser, Laerke, OUP 2022) is cited in
secondary sources but not yet indexed in CrossRef; it appears as an
INCOMPLETE entry below.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The synthesis should organise around three moments: (1) the
appropriationist tradition (Rorty--Bennett); (2) the contextualist
challenge (Skinner, with Garber as its main philosophical proxy); and
(3) recent attempts to transcend the binary or articulate hybrid
methods (Mercer, Della Rocca, Laerke, Corkum, Hangai). Ferreira da
Silva 2022 provides a useful meta-level map of the debate from an
unusual standpoint (Brentano).

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Appropriationism/Rational Reconstruction: 5 papers -- Rorty 1984,
  Della Rocca 2015, Prescott-Couch, Beaney 2013, Shapiro 2016
- Contextualism: 5 papers -- Skinner 1969, Garber 2005, Mercer 2019,
  Lamb 2009, Hangai 2023
- Hybrid/third-way: 5 papers -- Antognazza 2015, Blau 2020, Corkum
  2023, Ferreira da Silva 2022, Laerke 2019
- Foundational/background: 3 papers -- Rorty et al. (eds.) 1984,
  Laerke/Smith/Schliesser (eds.) 2013, Frede 2021
====================================================================
}

@book{rortySchneewindSkinner1984philosophyHistory,
  editor = {Rorty, Richard and Schneewind, Jerome B. and Skinner, Quentin},
  title = {Philosophy in History},
  year = {1984},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511625534},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This edited volume collects foundational essays on how
  philosophers should engage with the philosophical past, including
  Rorty's programmatic "The Historiography of Philosophy: Four Genres"
  (pp. 49-76), which distinguishes rational reconstruction, historical
  reconstruction, Geistesgeschichte, and doxography. Rorty argues that
  rational reconstruction -- treating past thinkers as having answers
  to our questions -- is legitimate as one genre alongside, not instead
  of, historical reconstruction.

  RELEVANCE: This volume is the locus classicus for the framing of the
  appropriationism/contextualism debate in Anglophone philosophy;
  virtually all subsequent methodological literature responds to it.
  Rorty's four-genre typology has structured the debate for four
  decades.

  POSITION: The volume establishes the pluralist framing that both
  sides of the debate have subsequently contested or defended.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, appropriationism, contextualism, rational-reconstruction, foundational, High},
  abstract = {The sixteen essays in this volume confront the current debate about the relationship between philosophy and its history. On the one hand intellectual historians commonly accuse philosophers of writing bad - anachronistic - history of philosophy, and on the other, philosophers have accused intellectual historians of writing bad - antiquarian - history of philosophy. The essays here address this controversy and ask what purpose the history of philosophy should serve. Part I contains more purely theoretical and methodological discussion, of such questions as whether there are 'timeless' philosophical problems, whether the issues of one epoch are commensurable with those of another, and what style is appropriate to the historiography of the subject. The essays in Part II consider a number of case-histories. They present important revisionist scholarship and original contributions on topics drawn from ancient, early modern and more recent philosophy. All the essays have been specially commissioned, and the contributors include many of the leading figures in the field. The volume as a whole will be of vital interest to everyone concerned with the study of philosophy and of its history.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{rorty1984historiography,
  author = {Rorty, Richard},
  title = {The historiography of philosophy: four genres},
  booktitle = {Philosophy in History},
  year = {1984},
  pages = {49--76},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511625534.006},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rorty distinguishes four genres of writing about
  philosophical history: rational reconstruction (making past
  philosophers interlocutors in current debates), historical
  reconstruction (situating texts in their context), Geistesgeschichte
  (locating philosophy in broader intellectual culture), and
  doxography (textbook summaries). He defends rational reconstruction
  as philosophically fruitful while acknowledging it distorts the
  historical record; the two genres serve different but compatible
  purposes. The essay introduces the vocabulary of "appropriation"
  that has since defined one pole of the debate.

  RELEVANCE: This is the canonical statement of the appropriationist
  position, and the primary target of contextualist responses by
  Garber, Mercer, and others. Any treatment of the debate must engage
  directly with this text.

  POSITION: Appropriationism/rational reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, appropriationism, rational-reconstruction, Rorty, High},
  abstract = {Analytic philosophers who have attempted ‘rational reconstructions’ of the arguments of great dead philosophers have done so in the hope of treating these philosophers as contemporaries, as colleagues with whom they can exchange views. They have argued that unless one does this one might as well turn over the history of philosophy to historians – whom they picture as mere doxographers, rather than seekers after philosophical truth. Such reconstructions, however, have led to charges of anachronism. Analytic historians of philosophy are frequently accused of beating texts into the shape of propositions currently being debated in the philosophical journals. It is urged that we should not force Aristotle or Kant to take sides in current debates within philosophy of language or metaethics. There seems to be a dilemma: either we anachronistically impose enough of our problems and vocabulary on the dead to make them conversational partners, or we confine our interpretive activity to making their falsehoods look less silly by placing them in the context of the benighted times in which they were written.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{skinner1969meaningUnderstanding,
  author = {Skinner, Quentin},
  title = {Meaning and Understanding in the History of Ideas},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1969},
  volume = {8},
  number = {1},
  pages = {3},
  doi = {10.2307/2504188},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Skinner attacks three "mythologies" that distort the
  history of ideas: the mythology of doctrines (reading a thinker as
  having a doctrine on questions they never addressed), the mythology
  of coherence (assuming that any thinker must hold a consistent
  system), and the mythology of prolepsis (treating earlier thinkers
  as anticipating later ones). Against these, Skinner argues that
  understanding a text requires recovering the author's intention in
  the context of the specific language games available to them; meaning
  is tied to illocutionary acts performed within a linguistic context.

  RELEVANCE: This article is the founding document of the Cambridge
  School of contextualism and the primary source for arguments against
  appropriationist history of philosophy. Skinner's three mythologies
  are the main charges appropriationists must answer.

  POSITION: Contextualism -- the locus classicus of the view.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, contextualism, Skinner, methodology, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{garber2005whatPhilosophical,
  author = {Garber, Daniel},
  title = {What Is Philosophical about the History of Philosophy?},
  booktitle = {Analytic Philosophy and History of Philosophy},
  year = {2005},
  pages = {129--146},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/oso/9780199278992.003.0007},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Garber defends a contextualist approach to the
  history of philosophy by arguing that past philosophers were
  addressing different questions with different conceptual resources,
  and that imposing contemporary questions produces philosophical
  distortion. He distinguishes "philosophy done historically" from
  "history done philosophically" and aligns himself with the former:
  understanding past philosophy on its own terms is itself a
  philosophical activity because it reveals the contingency of our
  current philosophical assumptions.

  RELEVANCE: Garber is the most prominent philosopher-historian to
  maintain the contextualist position explicitly against Bennett-style
  rational reconstruction; his exchange with Della Rocca (JHP 2015)
  is the proximate occasion for Mercer's retrospective on the
  "contextualist revolution." This chapter is an early statement of
  his mature view.

  POSITION: Contextualism within philosophy proper (as opposed to
  historical scholarship).
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, contextualism, Garber, early-modern, High},
  abstract = {Abstract Virtually every fellow historian of philosophy I know would agree to the proposition that analytic philosophers should take the history of philosophy seriously. But, at the same time, virtually every historian of philosophy I know has at one time or another been involved in a heated conversation on the subject, and been forced to defend the philosophical relevance of the history of philosophy to a benighted colleague who simply can’t see the point.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{dellaRocca2015interpretingSpinoza,
  author = {Della Rocca, Michael},
  title = {Interpreting Spinoza: The Real is the Rational},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {53},
  number = {3},
  pages = {523--535},
  doi = {10.1353/hph.2015.0049},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Della Rocca defends an unapologetically
  appropriationist reading of Spinoza, arguing that the philosopher's
  metaphysics is best understood by deploying our current best
  understanding of rationalist principles rather than by contextualising
  Spinoza among his contemporaries. He argues that context can mislead:
  what matters is whether an argument is valid and whether the
  conclusions follow, not what Spinoza's historical interlocutors
  believed. This is the appropriationist pole of the 2015 JHP debate
  with Garber.

  RELEVANCE: Together with Garber's response in the same issue, this
  article provides the most focused recent confrontation between
  appropriationism and contextualism in the history of early modern
  philosophy, making it the natural anchor text for the debate section
  of the synthesis.

  POSITION: Appropriationism/rational reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, appropriationism, Della-Rocca, Spinoza, High},
  abstract = {Interpreting Spinoza:The Real is the Rational Michael Della Rocca (bio) in his characteristically generous and searching discussion of my book, Spinoza, Daniel Garber rightly points out that I structure my interpretation of Spinoza's system around the principle of sufficient reason (the PSR). This is the principle that, as I and others sometimes put it, each fact has an explanation and is thus not brute, or the principle that each thing has an explanation. The 'or' will soon be important. Indeed, it might seem that I am too focused on the PSR—certainly I seem that way to Garber1—for I seek to use the PSR to unlock any number of problems that interpreters of Spinoza have faced over the last three centuries. Garber does a great job of conveying the range of uses to which I put the PSR in an attempt to bring Spinoza's system under control, so I will not go into the details except to say that the interpretation I offer covers not only Spinoza's metaphysics, but also his epistemology, philosophy of mind, psychology, moral and political philosophy, philosophy of religion, and, in a way, his account of human salvation. I also will not talk about the crucial two-fold use to which Spinoza puts the PSR and which leads to some of the idealist strands of my reading. Garber omits any discussion of the two-fold use, and so I will leave it out here too. The general point is that, for me, the PSR opens up breathtaking interpretive vistas that reveal Spinoza's system to be coherent, defensible, and groundbreaking in unexpected ways. It is an exciting story, one that I was and am happy to tell. Garber's aim in his essay is to challenge my reading of Spinoza both on first-order interpretive grounds and on second-order methodological grounds. With regard to the first-order worries, Garber's main points are that there is no good evidence that Spinoza is committed to the PSR in the strong—"no brute facts"—form in which I present it and that Spinoza does in fact allow at least one major brute fact at the heart of his system. With regard to the second-order worries, Garber expresses a preference for what he sees as a "direct reading" of Spinoza over what he sees as my rational reconstructions of Spinoza. His Spinoza is the [End Page 523] "real historical" Spinoza, whereas mine is an ideal type, a superhero but not the actual philosopher. I would like to push back a bit against Garber on both the first- and second-order grounds. With regard to the first-order worries, I reaffirm my textually well-grounded reasons for seeing Spinoza as espousing a strong form of the PSR, and I also offer reasons for denying that there is the big brute fact that Garber finds in Spinoza's system. With regard to Garber's second-order, methodological worries, my main aim will be to try to characterize more accurately some genuine methodological differences between us and to stress that both Garber's approach and my own are valuable ways of getting at the real Spinoza. Before turning to the first-order skirmishes and second-order methodological love fest, I would like to offer two observations about the way in which Garber frames his discussion of my book and of my approach to Spinoza. First, at the outset, Garber compares the single-mindedness of my PSR-focused perspective on Spinoza both to the single-mindedness of Russell's interpretation of Leibniz's philosophy as motivated by his logic2 and to the single-mindedness of Bernard Williams's interpretation of the Descartes of the Meditations as a "pure enquirer."3 I am, of course, only too happy for my work on Spinoza to be mentioned in the same paragraph (or book or library!) as these classic works by Russell and Williams; and, however inapt the comparison would be in other respects, I regard Garber as correct in pointing out similarities between the apparently unified interpretations at work in my book and in these others. I would also say that many other works on historical...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mercer2019contextualistRevolution,
  author = {Mercer, Christia},
  title = {The Contextualist Revolution in Early Modern Philosophy},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {57},
  number = {3},
  pages = {529--548},
  doi = {10.1353/hph.2019.0057},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mercer surveys what she calls the "silent revolution"
  in early modern philosophy scholarship since the 1980s, by which
  contextualism replaced rational reconstructionism as the dominant
  methodology. She traces this shift through changes in scholarly
  practice, identifies the "getting things right constraint" (GTRC)
  as the methodological norm now broadly shared by contextualists, and
  argues that this revolution has been productive while also creating
  new challenges around the expanding corpus and the need for
  philosophical judgment.

  RELEVANCE: This paper is the most systematic recent assessment of
  the debate's outcome within early modern studies, providing the
  synthesis writer with a retrospective framing. Mercer's GTRC concept
  is a useful handle for discussing what contextualists agree on.

  POSITION: Contextualism -- retrospective endorsement and
  complication of the contextualist turn.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, contextualism, early-modern, Mercer, High},
  abstract = {This paper examines the current state of early modern scholarship. After tracing the contours of the contextualist revolution that gained momentum in the 1980s, I argue that rational reconstructionism is a thing of the past and that early modernists are now devoted to employ all available skills, both philosophical and scholarly, to maximize our understanding of an increasingly broad range of texts and topics. I propose that our current differences concern the kinds of projects we select and the skills we use to maximize our understanding.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{antognazza2015benefit,
  author = {Antognazza, Maria Rosa},
  title = {The Benefit to Philosophy of the Study of Its History},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {23},
  number = {1},
  pages = {161--184},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2014.974020},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Antognazza argues that history of philosophy is both
  a kind of history and a kind of philosophy, and that deep engagement
  with the philosophical past produces distinctive philosophical
  benefits: it trains the mind to think outside current orthodoxies,
  exposes the contingency of current categories, and shows that
  "latest is not always best." Far from imprisoning philosophers in
  dead views, historical study has an innovative and subversive
  potential.

  RELEVANCE: This paper provides a nuanced philosophical defense of
  historicist practice without fully collapsing into either pole:
  Antognazza preserves normative philosophical engagement while insisting
  on historical rigor. It represents a "both/and" position useful for
  the hybrid/third-way section of the synthesis.

  POSITION: Hybrid -- philosophy and history are not in tension but
  mutually enriching.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, methodology, hybrid, Antognazza, High},
  abstract = {This paper advances the view that the history of philosophy is both a kind of history and a kind of philosophy. Through a discussion of some examples from epistemology, metaphysics, and the historiography of philosophy, it explores the benefit to philosophy of a deep and broad engagement with its history. It comes to the conclusion that doing history of philosophy is a way to think outside the box of the current philosophical orthodoxies. Somewhat paradoxically, far from imprisoning its students in outdated and crystallized views, the history of philosophy trains the mind to think differently and alternatively about the fundamental problems of philosophy. It keeps us alert to the fact that latest is not always best, and that a genuinely new perspective often means embracing and developing an old insight. The upshot is that the study of the history of philosophy has an innovative and subversive potential, and that philosophy has a great deal to gain from a long, broad, and deep conversation with its history.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

